WEBVTT

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:03.850
A battery can be most of the way full and
still be finished for the thing it powers.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:08.410
The gadget resets, the screen goes dark,
and the cell can still have plenty inside.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:10.450
So what, exactly, ran out?

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.110
In that case, it was not the charge.

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.270
I'm Tanmay Kulkarni, in
for Humanitarians AI.

00:00:16.660 --> 00:00:18.070
I did not open a real battery.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:22.410
I built a model of a C R twenty thirty-two
coin cell from two manufacturers'

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.690
published graphs. Then I asked
it one question again and again:

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:28.450
how much is still left at
the moment a device resets?

00:00:29.467 --> 00:00:31.917
First, why a battery's
voltage drops at all.

00:00:32.107 --> 00:00:35.737
A coin cell is a 3 volt source with
a small resistor hidden inside it.

00:00:35.827 --> 00:00:39.337
Energizer, a maker of these cells,
says a fresh one starts near 10 ohms.

00:00:39.427 --> 00:00:40.837
When a device asks for current,

00:00:40.857 --> 00:00:43.317
some of the voltage is spent pushing
that current through the resistor,

00:00:43.337 --> 00:00:45.237
and what is left is what the device sees.

00:00:45.627 --> 00:00:49.197
Ask for 30 milliamps, and a fresh cell
loses about three tenths of a volt.

00:00:49.667 --> 00:00:52.157
Voltage at the terminals
equals the open voltage,

00:00:52.727 --> 00:00:54.597
minus the current times the resistance.

00:00:56.733 --> 00:00:58.623
Now give the device a reset line.

00:00:58.713 --> 00:01:00.383
Below some voltage, it restarts.

00:01:00.533 --> 00:01:03.203
I chose 2 volts for mine.
That turns into a limit.

00:01:03.373 --> 00:01:06.063
The largest burst the cell
can pass is the open voltage,

00:01:06.083 --> 00:01:08.083
minus 2 volts, divided by the resistance.

00:01:08.633 --> 00:01:11.663
A fresh cell, at 10 ohms,
can pass about 100 milliamps.

00:01:11.773 --> 00:01:14.423
Anything larger, and the
device resets on the first try.

00:01:14.633 --> 00:01:17.803
So there is a line, and your burst
either sits under it or it does not.

00:01:19.300 --> 00:01:20.730
Here is what made me sit up.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:22.750
The resistor does not stay 10 ohms.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:25.970
Energizer's own plot, from a
pulse test of about 30 milliamps,

00:01:25.990 --> 00:01:27.710
shows it climbing as the cell is used.

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:30.790
As I read it: about 14 ohms,
then nineteen, then twenty-eight,

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:31.410
then forty-five,

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.490
and about 65 at the very
end. So the line comes down.

00:01:34.510 --> 00:01:36.170
At the start, about 100 milliamps.

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:38.710
150 milliamp-hours in, about 43.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:40.530
Near the end, about 8.5.

00:01:40.940 --> 00:01:43.290
The cell is the same cell the whole time.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.210
What it can safely give is not.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:47.550
Now I make three asks.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:50.710
First, a small one: 8 milliamps.
I ran it all the way down.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:01:52.010
It never crosses the line.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:55.290
Even at the very end of the plotted
run, the line is only just above eight.

00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:58.650
So in my model the device uses the
whole cell, and nothing is stranded.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:02.990
At 10 milliamps, the cell is nearly
spent when it resets, with about 1% left.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.050
A small ask spends almost everything.

00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:08.317
Second, a medium ask: 30 milliamps.

00:02:08.527 --> 00:02:10.037
Now the falling line meets it.

00:02:10.127 --> 00:02:14.337
It happens at about 174 milliamp-hours
used, out of a plotted 208.

00:02:14.627 --> 00:02:18.437
The device resets with about
16.5% of the cell unspent.

00:02:18.587 --> 00:02:22.157
Roughly one part in six, locked
inside a cell that tests fine.

00:02:23.167 --> 00:02:25.337
Third, a large ask: 60 milliamps.

00:02:25.487 --> 00:02:28.017
This one meets the line at
about 108 milliamp-hours.

00:02:28.407 --> 00:02:31.917
The device resets with almost
half the cell unspent, 48%.

00:02:31.937 --> 00:02:36.057
And at 100 milliamps, a fresh cell resets
on the first burst, with all of it left.

00:02:36.207 --> 00:02:38.617
Same cell, three asks, and
three different endings.

00:02:39.733 --> 00:02:41.723
Here is the rule, and
you can do it on paper.

00:02:41.993 --> 00:02:44.803
The largest burst a cell can
pass is the open voltage,

00:02:44.823 --> 00:02:47.423
minus the reset voltage,
divided by the resistance.

00:02:47.953 --> 00:02:49.683
Let's try one together, with an old cell:

00:02:49.773 --> 00:02:53.403
its resistance is 40 ohms, and its
open voltage is two point seven volts.

00:02:53.633 --> 00:02:56.023
Two point seven, minus
two, is point seven.

00:02:56.043 --> 00:02:59.523
Point seven, divided by forty,
is about 17.5 milliamps.

00:02:59.693 --> 00:03:01.823
Any burst over that,
and the device resets,

00:03:02.233 --> 00:03:04.423
however much charge is left. Check it:

00:03:04.493 --> 00:03:07.843
a device asking for 25 milliamps
resets on that old cell,

00:03:07.863 --> 00:03:09.643
and one asking for ten does not.

00:03:10.173 --> 00:03:13.523
Now do yours, by looking up two
numbers in your device's data sheet:

00:03:13.613 --> 00:03:16.783
its biggest current draw, and the
voltage where its chip resets.

00:03:17.800 --> 00:03:20.070
I pushed on the model
to see what moves it.

00:03:20.090 --> 00:03:22.730
Raise the reset line from 2 volts to 2.4,

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:26.290
and the thirty milliamp ask strands
42%, not sixteen and a half.

00:03:26.740 --> 00:03:28.650
Energizer's handbook makes the same point:

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:32.730
a device whose cutoff is above 2 volts
may leave significant capacity unused.

00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:37.530
Use a resistance curve from a very slow
drain, and the same ask strands about 40%.

00:03:37.550 --> 00:03:39.290
And one cell is not every cell.

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:43.290
If I let resistance vary by
about 15% from cell to cell,

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:47.910
most cells at 30 milliamps land between
about eleven and twenty-three percent.

00:03:48.900 --> 00:03:50.610
Let me be plain about what this is.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.610
It is a model, fitted to graphs
I read off two data sheets,

00:03:53.630 --> 00:03:56.730
so every number is about, not
exact. I tested no real cell.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:59.410
I did not model cold, which
makes the voltage drop worse.

00:03:59.720 --> 00:04:03.690
I did not model a capacitor. And the
reset line is my own choice, not a chip's.

00:04:03.710 --> 00:04:06.890
What holds is the shape: the bigger
the ask, the more is left behind.

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:11.710
So the next time something resets
with a battery that still reads fine,

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:13.630
do not ask how much charge is left.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:16.810
Ask how big its biggest burst
is, and where the line sits.

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:20.470
A cell can die rich. How rich
depends on what you ask of it.
